// How settled a country is, and what decides it.
//
// Stability is no longer a number that drifts. Every region has one of its own, worked out
// from what it lives on, what it thinks, who its people are and whose soldiers are standing
// in it; the country's is what those add up to, plus what the war is costing and what the
// country is beyond its conditions.
//
// The capital counts for far more than its size. That is the historically honest part - a
// state survives a furious province and falls to a furious capital - but it is a choice,
// and `capital_share` is where to disagree with it.
//
// A region's stability, 0 to 1:
//
//     base
//     + (standard_of_living - living_tolerance) * living
//     - radicalism        * radicalism
//     - foreign_share     * minority
//     + (literacy - 0.5)  * literacy
//     - occupied_share    * occupation
//
// And the country's:
//
//     capital_region      * capital_share
//   + rest by population  * (1 - capital_share)
//   - (war + (1 - war_support) * war_support)     while it is at war
//   + the country's own baseline
//
// That last term is set once when the scenario starts, to the difference between the
// stability the scenario authored and what conditions alone explain. It is everything the
// formula does not model - institutions, legitimacy, a century of habit - and it is what
// keeps switching to a formula from restating the settledness of all 261 countries on the
// first week. Government, ideology, cabinet and budget effects still accumulate into it,
// which is why a decade of good government eventually shows.
{
  "base": 0.62,

  "living": 0.55,
  "living_tolerance": 0.45,

  "radicalism": 0.85,
  "minority": 0.30,
  "occupation": 0.55,
  "literacy": 0.04,

  "capital_share": 0.35,

  // What a war costs the state that is fighting it, and what a war nobody wants any more
  // costs on top.
  //
  // These were 0.06 and 0.10, which put a ceiling of 0.16 on the entire cost of a war - and
  // that ceiling was the reason a country could lose a war, be occupied, watch its war
  // support fall through the floor and still sit at 0.70 stability, permanently above the
  // civil-war line. Measured: after five years of the 2024 world, the tenth percentile of
  // stability was 0.42 against a civil-war threshold of 0.32, and not one new civil war had
  // begun anywhere - the four in the world were the four the scenario authored.
  //
  // A war the people still believe in is now a real cost but a survivable one (0.14 plus a
  // fifth of 0.40). A war they have stopped believing in is 0.44, which takes a country from
  // 0.70 to 0.26 - below the line - and that is the causal chain that was missing: Russia in
  // 1917, Germany in 1918, Austria-Hungary, the Ottomans. Defeat is how a state comes apart,
  // and it has to actually cost that much for the mechanic to mean anything.
  "war": 0.14,
  "war_support": 0.40,

  // What it costs to have someone else's army standing in the region. Raised with the war
  // terms for the same reason: occupied ground is the state visibly not governing, and it was
  // priced as a footnote.
  "occupation": 0.68,

  // How much of the gap to its target a country closes in a week. A tenth means a shock
  // takes a season to arrive rather than a week, which is about how fast a country
  // actually comes apart.
  "approach": 0.10,

  // The most that how a country is being governed can be worth, either way, and how much
  // of it is given back each week. Bounded on purpose: a cabinet should be able to steady a
  // country, not to make it permanently unfallable - and a bad decade should be something a
  // country can come out of.
  "governance_limit": 0.15,
  "governance_decay": 0.004,

  "floor": 0.05,
  "ceiling": 0.98
}
